# Line endings belong to the release, not to the checkout.
#
# The v1.0.0 bundle records a SHA-256 for every artifact, and gate 31 verifies
# that the bundle alone reconstructs what it claims. Those digests are taken
# over the file as it sits in the working tree, so a clone that rewrites LF to
# CRLF -- the default for Git for Windows with core.autocrlf=true -- fails them
# on a tree where nothing has changed. Verified by converting one artifact and
# watching RECONSTRUCTION FAILED.
#
# Two release gates have already been repaired for reading a carriage return as
# content: build-spec-index.sh took the CR as part of a status word, and
# api-baseline.sh read it as part of every symbol name and reported the entire
# public API as deleted. Stripping at each reader treats the symptom one site at
# a time. This removes the cause: every text file is LF in every working tree on
# every platform, so no gate and no checksum can depend on which operating
# system ran the clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Captures are measurements. No filter goes near one.
*.wav binary

# Serial logs are deliberately NOT marked binary, and the reason should be on
# the record rather than inferred. A board's terminal frames its lines with
# CRLF, but the measurement is the text those lines carry, not the framing --
# and every such log in these repositories was already stored LF before this
# file existed. Marking them binary now would not recover a carriage return
# that was never committed; it would only make the archived record disagree
# with itself. The measurements that must survive byte-for-byte are the
# captures, and those are binary above.

# Preserve exact provenance when source is exported without .git.
.git-archive-revision export-subst
